The 1-RTT packet numbers a client leaves out, on quic-go's schedule.

Both stacks skip a packet number now and then, so that a peer acknowledging a packet it never got
gives itself away. quic-go's skippingPacketNumberGenerator
(vendor/quic-go/internal/ackhandler/packet_number_generator.go:36-84) leaves out the next number
to be used plus 3 plus a draw below twice a period, and the period starts at 256 and doubles after
each draw up to 128 Ki (SkipPacketInitialPeriod, SkipPacketMaxPeriod,
vendor/quic-go/internal/protocol/params.go:45-50); the draw is four bytes of crypto/rand, masked
(vendor/quic-go/internal/utils/rand.go:13-29). Upstream ngtcp2 put the next skip (r + 1) * 2^k + 3
on, for one random byte r and a k growing from 0 without a ceiling: its first skip fell in
3..258 where quic-go's falls in 3..514, and its gaps kept doubling past quic-go's 256 Ki.

Only the node sees packet numbers — they are inside header protection — but it sees all of them.
A client now runs conn_qgo_update_skip_pkt; the server keeps upstream's schedule.

Checked against quic-go's own generator, run by tools/qgovec on the same random bytes: test/ngpatch.

